Plantar Fasciitis
Musculoskeletal & Pain
First-step morning heel pain, Medial calcaneal tubercle tenderness, Positive Windlass test

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-043
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of heel pain causing a sudden, total inability to bear weight when rising from a seated position, leading to a near-fall.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient who experienced an agonizing, sharp stabbing pain in the heel upon standing after a prolonged period of rest, causing their leg to give way and resulting in severe distress and immobility. Urgent assessment was required to rule out an acute rupture of the plantar fascia or calcaneal stress fracture, and to provide immediate mechanical offloading and analgesia to restore safe, basic ambulation prior to the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I sat down to watch a movie, and when I stood up, it felt like I stepped on a three-inch nail; my heel gave out and I hit the floor."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute, functionally limiting flare of chronic right heel pain. Timeline: Insidious onset of severe, sharp, stabbing pain localized exactly to the medial heel over the past 3 months. Patient reports the classic hallmark: maximal pain intensity is experienced upon taking the very first steps out of bed in the morning, which rapidly improves after 10 minutes of hobbling. Tonight, after resting for 2 hours on the sofa, the patient stood up and experienced an explosive exacerbation of this "first-step" pain, leading to sudden limb yielding. The patient is currently terrified to put the heel on the ground.
Functional Status: Ambulating by severely hobbling on the forefoot/toes; completely refusing to let the heel strike the floor.
Social/Safety Concerns: Fall risk due to severe, unpredictable pain spikes upon initial weight-bearing.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 138/86 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 90 Kg
General: Alert, visibly anxious about standing, sitting with the right foot resting on the contralateral knee.
Physical Exam: Inspection reveals a complete absence of overt external swelling, massive erythema, or systemic inflammatory signs on the heel or foot. Exquisite, focal point tenderness is palpated directly at the origin of the plantar fascia on the medial tubercle of the calcaneus. Distinctly positive Windlass test: passive, forceful dorsiflexion of the toes severely exacerbates the heel pain and makes the fascial band taut and tender. Decreased ankle dorsiflexion range of motion is noted, secondary to a very tight Achilles/calf complex. Palpation along the arch reveals a tight fascial band, but no palpable defect or massive localized hematoma to suggest a complete acute rupture.
Environmental Observations: Hardwood floors throughout the house without rugs; patient is barefoot.
Medication Reconciliation: Took Paracetamol 1g with no relief of the sharp, mechanical tension pain.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute mechanical flare of Plantar Fasciitis - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute Plantar Fascia Rupture - Ruled out clinically by the absence of a sudden "pop" followed by a palpable gap in the arch, lack of massive acute bruising, and the classic, chronic "first-step" history.

Calcaneal Stress Fracture - Ruled out by the pain being exquisitely localized to the fascial origin (plantar surface) rather than eliciting severe pain on a medial-lateral calcaneal squeeze test.

Tarsal Tunnel Syndrome - Ruled out by the absence of burning, tingling, or numbness radiating into the toes, and a negative Tinel's sign over the posterior tibial nerve.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with a severe, immobilizing exacerbation of classic plantar fasciitis, triggered by the "post-static dyskinesia" phenomenon (intense pain after a period of rest). The critical clinical step was differentiating this severe mechanical tension pain from an acute fascial rupture or a stress fracture. Physical examination clearly localized the pathology to the fascial insertion (Windlass test, focal tenderness) and ruled out structural failure. The sudden inability to weight-bear safely rendered the patient highly distressed and at risk of a fall on their hardwood floors. Urgent after-hours assessment provided diagnostic reassurance, bypassed the need for emergency X-rays, and allowed for the immediate implementation of mechanical taping, supportive footwear advice, and acute inflammatory management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Ibuprofen 400mg TDS with food for 5-7 days to aggressively manage the insertion-site inflammation. Applied a firm rigid tape (Low-Dye taping technique) to physically support the plantar arch.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Keep a pair of highly cushioned, supportive running shoes directly next to the bed and sofa; put them on before taking a single step. Consequence: The shoe absorbs the shock and the built-in arch physically prevents the inflamed fascia from aggressively tearing under body weight, immediately stopping the "nail-in-the-heel" pain (negative reinforcement).
Action: Roll the arch of the foot firmly over a frozen water bottle for 15 minutes at the end of every day. Consequence: The ice constricts the inflamed tissue while the bottle physically stretches and massages the tight band, leading to significantly less stiffness the following morning (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Podiatry referral for long-term orthotic management and biomechanical assessment.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A (Taping applied).
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2-3 weeks to monitor progress.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the heel suddenly becomes massively swollen, hot, and red, if a fever develops, or if there is a sudden, loud "pop" in the arch followed by a complete inability to walk.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical, representing severe inflammation and micro-tearing of the foot's main support band. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The sharp, stabbing pain will become a dull ache within a few days with the taping and shoes. However, complete resolution of plantar fasciitis often takes 6-12 months of consistent calf stretching and strict footwear modification.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if the bottom of your foot suddenly bruises massively, or if you feel a loud pop in your arch and cannot put any weight on it at all.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The thick band supporting your arch shrinks and tightens when you sit or sleep. When you suddenly stand up, your body weight violently stretches that tight, inflamed band, causing microscopic tearing at the heel bone (the "nail" feeling). The rigid tape I applied acts as an artificial arch, stopping it from stretching too far.
Actionable Steps: Absolutely no barefoot walking on the hardwood floors, starting right now. You must step directly into supportive, cushioned shoes when getting off the sofa or out of bed. Roll your foot on a frozen water bottle to ice and stretch the band simultaneously.

